    {"slug": "gr-u4-1", "text": "We can use comparative adjectives to compare two people or things.", "voice": "F"},
    {"slug": "gr-u4-2", "text": "Tom is taller than Mary.", "voice": "F"},
    {"slug": "gr-u4-3", "text": "A house in a city is normally more expensive than a house in the countryside.", "voice": "F"},
    {"slug": "gr-u5-1", "text": "Countable nouns are for the people and things we can count using numbers.", "voice": "F"},
    {"slug": "gr-u5-2", "text": "Countable nouns can be singular: a rock, an island, or plural: rocks, islands.", "voice": "F"},
    {"slug": "gr-u5-3", "text": "Uncountable nouns are for the things that we cannot count with numbers.", "voice": "F"},
    {"slug": "gr-u5-4", "text": "They usually do not have a plural form: cream, chocolate.", "voice": "F"},
    {"slug": "gr-u5-5", "text": "We use some, many, a few with countable nouns.", "voice": "F"},
    {"slug": "gr-u5-6", "text": "We use some, much, a little with uncountable nouns.", "voice": "F"},
    {"slug": "gr-u6-3", "text": "My mother bought some fruits.", "voice": "F"},
